{
  "abstract": "No rational Diophantine septuple is known, and none has been ruled out. We give height-certified negative results near known sextuples. For a triple inside a tuple, the rationals extending the triple form a coset $P_0+2E(\\mathbb{Q})$ on an elliptic curve. We use a provably complete Mordell–Weil basis: PARI ellrank gives matching rank bounds, and eclib saturation at all primes returns index 1. We enumerate every lattice point up to a canonical-height radius and sieve the remaining square conditions modulo about 30 primes. Survivors are checked exactly. Silverman's bound turns the radius into an explicit naive-height bound $B$: every extension of logarithmic height at most $B$ is found. We cover 759 sextuples: 692 from the literature or a closure step, 66 from the parametric family of Dujella et al., and one new one. None has a seventh element with height at most $B$, where $B\\ge 2477$ for the 692, i.e. numerators and denominators up to $10^{1075}$. For all 4130 sub-quintuples of the 692, the sixth elements are exactly as known ($B\\ge 1526$), and the 22 quintuples with two sixth elements are exactly the 22 near-septuples. For 11146 of the 11274 sub-quadruples of the 759 sextuples, the compatibility graph on the fifth elements with height at most $B$ ($B\\ge 414$) has no triangle, and every edge is a known sextuple. These censuses found a sextuple of height $1.96\\cdot 10^9$ and two almost-septuples, absent from all published lists we checked. They also found a fifth extension, missed by Zeng, of a published quadruple. On 876 quintuple curves the Mordell–Weil rank exceeds that of the span of the obvious points. All results are bounded by height and relative to the corpora; this is not a nonexistence proof.",
